we're joined in the studio now once  again by the health secretary sergeant  minister great to have you good morning  on the program um  let's just reflect of course plenty to  talk about in terms of your brief but  let's reflect on what we have seen  coming out of ukraine over the past 24  hours i personally have watched more  footage from ukraine than i would care  to ever admit to but this weekend seeing  children being pulled out of the rubble  kind of brought it home to me  is the british government yet to the  point where they can they can join  the ukrainian president ukrainian deputy  prime minister in describing what we're  seeing in ukraine as a as a genocide  look like everyone i'm appalled by the  the scenes coming out of ukraine right  now the despicable attacks on civilians  when i was last in your studio that was  a day we saw the picture of that the the  woman from a maternity hospital a  pregnant woman that'd be murdered uh by  the russians and now we're seeing  children being pulled out of rubble it's  in terms of um holding russia  accountable in russia and the president  putin he will absolutely be held  accountable for this  there's a there's an international  criminal court that will  work on this and they're already  compiling evidence the deputy prime  minister was in the hague last week  offering the uk's full support to hold  russia to account in the meantime we  will of course will continue to support  the ukrainians in every single way that  we can  i i do wonder though we heard from the  former prime minister gordon brown at  the weekend pointing out the problems  that there may well be with the  international criminal court process  least not least of which is there's not  a chance that we're going to be able to  extradite vladimir putin from russia to  face charges directly what do you make  of his suggestion that there is perhaps  a nuremberg style trial set up to try  and prosecute this which we can  prosecute in absentee if necessary look  i think all of that should be looked at  i think it's right that uh gordon brown  and others many people are thinking  about the the what ways and mechanisms  can the international community use to  hold russia to account i mean right here  and now i think it's really important  that we and our allies continue to  support russia in er because supporting  ukraine in in every way  with the military aid that we're  providing i think more than any other  country the humanitarian support that  we're providing as well all of this is  crucial but also making sure that russia  is held to account i i i understand that  this is not your brief i understand that  they're all cabinet ministers will want  to be careful with their language but  but i will note that you don't feel  comfortable going as far as the  as the ukrainian president and and being  literal about this stating that in your  view given what we have seen you feel  that he's committing accidents what  president zielenski thinks is very very  important to us we of course will listen  to him carefully the prime minister our  prime minister uh talks to him on a  regular basis and we will help the  ukrainians gather all the evidence they  need for successful prosecutions  whenever we can where are we at the  moment with the scheme to raise house  ukrainians coming to the united kingdom  um 3.4 million people have fled the  country at the moment and the concerns  are particularly from agencies working  in non-governmental organizations  working in this field is that we're not  being quick enough to match people up  well my understanding is with the so  there's two schemes at the moment with  the family reunification scheme i think  some nine and a half thousand visas have  been issued  with the hosting a ukrainian family  scheme i think some over 150 000 people  have registered their interest and the  process of matching those hosts with  with families from ukraine has begun  can i just ask about something that the  home secretary mentioned at the weekend  which i haven't really seen picked up  particularly widely we know that we have  had a different approach as regards  visas for ukrainians coming to this  country she said this the truth is that  a very small number of people can wreak  utter havoc and russia has a history of  covert hostile activity  are we really suggesting that we believe  that there could be russian agents  coming to this country under false  ukrainian passports and that's what the  home secretary is suggesting and she's  right too by the way to to point that  out  let's let's let's just what end would  russia be coming in in that fashion we  know the russians have done that they  did it in 2018 when i had her job  and we  we saw in our country russian agents  came here with a deadly nerve agent a  chemical weapon  and they used it in salisbury  we know it killed people and russia was  directly responsible for that they  infiltrated our country with agents with  a chemical weapon and used it and so it  is right there there are some level of  of security checks we also know that  extremists and extremist organizations  operate in that region of course the the  level of security checks has to be  proportionate uh to the the the issues  that we're dealing with the example that  you get in the same time  in the way of of letting ukrainians come  here to safety sure but the example you  gave of salisbury my understanding is  those two russians arrived on a russian  passport on a tourist visa  in what way would these these security  checks stop them coming well it wouldn't  be it wouldn't make sense to get into  detail about how our checks work uh  precisely but i think most people would  understand that we do what we can to  protect our homeland let you've been  very good so let's talk on speaking away  from your brief so let's move on to it  covered cases are clearly on the upswing  at the moment do you have an estimate of  when we might reach the peak  um i think with omicron  we have  we're past the peak now we've come down  from that level the although infections  are rising case numbers are rising and  hospitalizations have risen a bit as  well over the last two weeks they're  still way below their peak it's also  worth uh sort of keeping in mind why  we're seeing rises that was always going  to be the case when we opened up and  also we have a sub-variant let's call it  of the original omicron called ba2 which  we know on the one hand is more  infectious than the original omicron  variant but  thankfully these vaccines that we have  are just as effective against it  why is it taking so long to get to this  next stage of the rollout by my  reckoning we're about six months away  from the start of the third dose scheme  and we know of course that resistance  wins a lot quicker than you know within  six months should we perhaps have got to  this point slightly quicker or is it all  because of the jcvi no i think we're  exactly the the right places when it  comes to vaccine waning and  effectiveness it's something we monitor  almost on a daily basis i think as a  country we've probably done more  research on on that than any other  country but this is winning resistance  part of the reason why we are seeing  increased er infections increased i  think the the primary reasons are the  ones i mentioned which are increased uh  social mixing and uh also this ba2  subvariant the announcement today which  is a a spring booster dose  for those that are over 75 elderly  people living in care homes and the  immunosuppressed this will allow that  group of people which are at more  serious risk than others to top up their  protection against this virus and and  and give them that extra level of  confidence that they need is it not  somewhat counterintuitive at a point  which we're suggesting that over 75  people who are vulnerable should be able  to get a fourth dose that we're about a  fortnight away from the ending of free  covenant testing  um no i don't think the the two things  are necessarily related i mean our  primary  do you not think that if you remove free  testing fewer people will test  well obviously if there's fewer tests  around then than fuel will be used but  the the question really is do we still  need to have a universal free testing  offer which is as it sounds yes for  everyone whenever they want to test and  our determination based on the advice  that we receive is that is no longer  necessary we're in a much better stage  with kobe than we have been so far  clearly it's still there that's why we  still need to keep focusing on our  defenses but the best defense is  vaccination we've built a wall of  defense it's working in fact i think  this weekend the uk health security  agency they said in their analysis that  since december because of the booster  push that we had we've kept some 157  thousand people out of hospital so  vaccines work and that is why everyone  that's eligible please should come  forward  just just briefly should we expect a  broader rollout of a fourth dose well  the jcvi that's our group of independent  advisors they have said they are looking  at a autumn dose for over 50s but  there's no final decision at this point  in time can we just reflect just a  briefly um  going back to ukraine on the comments  that the prime minister made at the  weekend drawing a comparison in his mind  between  voting to leave the european union  and fighting against a russian invasion  of ukraine did you think it was  appropriate he seemed to suggest that  those who who didn't vote against brexit  like yourself were somehow not lovers of  freedom  well i i heard his comments and i don't  think he was suggesting that at all what  i heard from the prime minister was uh  the the basically the desire for  self-determination in in everyone no  matter what country they're in no matter  what their circumstance is strong i  don't think in any way he was connecting  the situations in ukraine and the uk and  if we want to know what the prime  minister thinks about ukraine and  responding i mean we can see for  ourselves in terms of the support that  he's provided you know rock solid  support compared to any other world  leader you don't you don't think it was  a tall crass for the prime minister to  make that comparison or to allude to a  comparison i mean the perp the brexit i  don't i don't think i don't accept that  he was comparing uk he mentioned one and  he mentioned the other in the series his  point his point was about  the importance of self-determination and  and people you know whoever they are  wherever they are they have this strong  feeling of wanting to have freedom and  determine i'm just i'm just generally  suggesting that the self-determination  you know shown by the brits who voted to  leave a political and economic union  it's not the same as the  self-determination shown by people who  are picking up guns and firing on  invaders and he wasn't saying i think  it's spurious to say that he was  connecting somehow  in that way well i i i think most normal  people listening to that wouldn't have  drawn that conclusion i think you can  see what he's talking about which is  just about the importance of  self-determination just just a final  point of course on all eyes this week on  what the chancellor does come come  wednesday and you i'm sure will not be  drawn on perhaps more money for your or  whatever else but just in terms of the  strategy currently isn't that isn't the  single biggest beneficiary of the rise  in the cost of living right now the the  treasury that any wiggle room that the  treasury has right now is down to  increase tax take at the petrol pumps  v80 and so on well look one of the  benefits of no longer being chancellor  is i don't really have to answer those  questions  sadie javits we'll leave it there thanks  for being with us thank you

